course: biology
lesson: 27
title: Dolphins, porpoises and seals
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A dolphin is a small toothed whale with one blowhole, about 80 to 100 cone-shaped teeth it never chews with, and a brain that can sleep one half at a time while the other half keeps watch and remembers to breathe.
explain: In murky water it «sees» with sound: it sends out fast clicks and listens to the echoes, so at an aquarium it can find a hidden ball even with soft cups over its eyes; this is called echolocation.
explain: Each bottlenose dolphin has its own signature whistle that works like a name, and friends call each other by copying it, a bit like shouting a mate's name across the playground.
explain: Seals are a different family, meat-eating mammals with four flippers that rest on rocks to save energy; sea lions can turn their back flippers forward and walk, while true seals wriggle on their bellies.
explain: Cartoons show a dolphin always smiling and a seal clapping and balancing a ball; the «smile» is just the shape of a dolphin's mouth, not a happy face, and the clapping seal is a trained sea lion, not what wild seals do.
fact: Dolphins find things by echolocation: they send out fast clicks and listen to the echoes, and sound travels well through water even where it is too muddy to see far.
fact: Bottlenose dolphins each have a signature whistle that works like a name, and they remember old friends' whistles for at least 20 years.
fact: Dolphins sleep with half of their brain at a time, switching sides, so they can keep coming up to breathe.
fact: Dolphins ride the waves made by boats and whales, which saves energy and seems to be fun for them.
fact: A bottlenose dolphin has around 80 to 100 cone-shaped teeth, gets only one set for life, and swallows fish whole.
fact: Some dolphins in Shark Bay, Australia, wear sea sponges on their beaks to protect them while digging for fish, a trick passed mostly from mothers to daughters.
fact: Porpoises are smaller cousins of dolphins with rounded heads, no long beak and spade-shaped teeth.
fact: The vaquita, a porpoise in Mexico, is the rarest sea mammal; around ten were reported in recent surveys.
fact: Seals haul out on rocks to rest, warm up and look after pups, and a thick layer of blubber keeps them warm in icy water.
fact: Sea lions have small outer ears, can turn their back flippers forward to walk, and swim with their front flippers; true seals have no outer ears and swim with their back flippers.
fact: A seal pup alone on a beach is usually waiting for its mother, who may not return if people come close, so stay well away, keep dogs on a lead and call a wildlife rescue only if it looks hurt.
fact: Elephant seals can dive over 1,500 metres deep and stay under for more than an hour, storing oxygen in their blood and muscles and slowing their heart.
fact: Seal whiskers are so sensitive that a blindfolded seal followed a fish's water trail that was more than 30 seconds old.
exercise: How does a dolphin find a hidden ball in murky water? | It clicks and listens to the echoes | It smells the ball under water | It feels the ball's heat | It waits for the ball to float up to the top
exercise: What is the dolphin's way of finding things by sound called? | Echolocation | Hibernation | Migration | Photosynthesis
exercise: What is a bottlenose dolphin's signature whistle? | A whistle that works like its name | A warning call that sharks are near | The sound it makes when it is hungry | A song that only males sing in spring
exercise: How long can dolphins remember the whistles of old friends? | At least 20 years | Only about 20 minutes | Only about 2 or 3 days | Only until the next tide
exercise: How does a dolphin sleep? | With one half of its brain at a time | Lying on the seabed all night | Floating upside down with both eyes shut | It never sleeps at all
exercise: Why can't a dolphin fall fully asleep like you? | It must keep coming up to breathe and watch for danger | Its eyes have no eyelids, so they can never close, even at night | The sea is too noisy | It is always too hungry
exercise: Why do dolphins ride the bow wave at the front of a boat? | It saves energy and seems to be fun | They are trying to stop the boat, or push it off course | They want food from the sailors | The boat's engine pulls them along
exercise: What does a dolphin do with its many pointy teeth? | Grips slippery fish and swallows them whole | Chews its food into tiny pieces, like we do, before swallowing | Grinds up seaweed | Digs holes in the sand
exercise: Why do some Shark Bay dolphins wear a sea sponge on the beak? | To protect it while digging on the rocky seafloor | To keep their beak warm in the cold water of winter | To hide from sharks | To soak up water to drink
exercise: How is a porpoise different from a dolphin? | It has a rounded head, no long beak and spade-shaped teeth | It is much bigger than a dolphin and has a very long, thin beak | It has gills and lays eggs | It lives only in rivers
exercise: Which is the rarest sea mammal? | The vaquita porpoise in Mexico | The bottlenose dolphin in Florida | The orca | The harbour seal
exercise: Why do seals lie on rocks for hours? | To rest, warm up and look after pups, saving energy | Because they are far too lazy to swim about in the cold sea all day | Because they can't swim at night | To dry out their gills
exercise: How can a sea lion walk on land? | It turns its back flippers forward under its body | It walks on its front flippers only | It hops on its tail | It can't, it just wriggles along flat on its belly like a worm
exercise: You find a fluffy seal pup alone on the beach. What should you do? | Stay well away, keep dogs on a lead, call wildlife rescue if it is hurt | Pick it up and carry it to the sea | Take it home and give it milk | Stroke it so it isn't lonely
exercise: How can an elephant seal stay under water for over an hour? | It stores lots of oxygen in its blood and muscles and slows its heart | It breathes through gills on its neck | It carries air bubbles in its nose | It sleeps the whole time under water and so needs almost no air
exercise: What are a harbour seal's long whiskers for? | Feeling the water trails left by swimming fish | Keeping its nose warm | Showing how old it is | Scaring off sharks and killer whales by poking them in the eye
test: flipper's always smiling so dolphins must be happy all the time right | No, the "smile" is just the shape of its mouth | Yes, dolphins are always happy | Yes, they smile when they're full | No, dolphins frown when the water's cold and smile only when it's warm
test: In the cartoon a seal claps its flippers and balances a ball. What's the real story? | That is a trick taught to sea lions, not what wild seals do | Wild seals do it every day on the rocks to have some fun | Only baby seals do it | Seals clap to call their mothers
test: Count them: how many flippers do three sea lions have altogether? | 12 | 6 | 9 | 3
test: My mate says porpoises are just baby dolphins. What do you say? | No, they are a separate group with rounded heads and spade-shaped teeth | Yes, they grow into dolphins | Yes, but only the grey ones | No, porpoises are a kind of fish that look a lot like dolphins
test: What would happen if a dolphin's whole brain fell asleep at once in deep water? | It might forget to come up and breathe | It would sink to the seabed and rest safely | It would breathe through its skin instead | Nothing, dolphins can breathe the water
test: Why do you think the sponge trick is called a kind of dolphin culture? | It is learned and passed on, mostly from mothers to daughters | All dolphins everywhere are born already knowing how to do it | The sponges teach the dolphins | It was taught by scientists
test: oi leave that seal pup alone, its mum's coming back. is that right? | Yes, the mother is usually just out fishing | No, a pup alone is always abandoned | Yes, but only if it's raining | No, seal mothers never come back once they leave the pup on the sand
test: A seal swims fast through dark water and catches a fish it can't see. Which sense helps most? | Touch, through its whiskers | Taste, through its flippers | Smell, under water | Sight, with night vision
test: A sea animal has small ear flaps and walks on its flippers at the zoo. What is it most likely? | A sea lion | A harbour seal | A young dolphin | A harbour porpoise
test: Why would sound be more useful than eyes for a dolphin hunting in a muddy river? | Sound travels well in water but you can't see far in mud | Dolphins are blind | Fish can't hear clicks | Eyes don't work in salt water, so dolphins never use them at all
test: Why do seals need a thick layer of blubber? | It keeps them warm in icy water | It makes them heavy enough to sink | It stores spare whiskers for later | It helps them walk about on land
exam: Identify the faculty by which a dolphin is enabled to locate objects in turbid water. | Echolocation, by means of emitted clicks and returning echoes | Olfaction, by means of the blowhole | Detection of heat by the skin | Vision alone, by means of eyes of unusual size and sensitivity
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manner in which the dolphin sleeps? | One hemisphere of the brain rests while the other remains awake | Both hemispheres rest at once upon the seabed | The animal does not sleep at any time | It sleeps only while stranded upon the shore, at the lowest of the tides
exam: Ascertain the course of action appropriate upon the discovery of an unaccompanied seal pup upon a beach. | Remain at a distance and notify a wildlife rescue if it appears injured | Convey it forthwith to the sea | Remove it to a private dwelling | Feed it with cow's milk
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the distinction between sea lions and true seals. | Sea lions possess external ears and can walk upon their flippers | True seals possess external ears and are able to walk upright upon land | Sea lions lack external ears entirely | The two are in every respect identical
